@using GroupSplit.Shared
@using GroupSplit.App.Shared.Extensions
@namespace GroupSplit.App.Shared.Components

@*
    How one expense is divided, for the two dialogs that write one.

    Three ways to answer, and one list of people whichever it is. That second part is the
    whole shape of this component and it was not always true: the editable fields lived here
    and a read-only "Everyone's share" list lived in the dialog underneath, so the moment
    somebody split by hand every member appeared twice -- once as a field they were typing
    in and once as a row echoing what they had just typed, four rows of chrome for two
    people. Now the list *is* the answer: editable when the person is stating it,
    read-only when the API worked it out.

    The choice used to be a switch whose label changed with its state, which is the one
    control nobody can read -- "Split it myself" beside a toggle tells you neither what is
    true now nor what clicking will do. Named options, one of them lit, says both.

    All for one is the middle answer and belongs with the automatic one rather than with
    the hand-typed one, whatever it looks like: it names a rule -- the one every group holds
    per member -- and the API divides by it. That is the difference from typing the whole
    amount against one person, and it is the difference that matters afterwards. Correcting
    the amount on an expense that named a rule divides it again by the same rule; correcting
    the amount on hand-typed shares is refused, because shares that summed to the old total
    do not sum to the new one.

    By hand sends the amounts shown, and they have to add up: the API refuses a set that
    does not, because nothing else re-derives the division and a set that does not sum
    makes every balance in the group wrong. The remaining line is what stands between
    somebody typing three numbers and meeting that refusal at the Save button.

    The other two state no amounts, and the dialog decides what that means -- which differs
    between the two of them, and is why the answer travels as null rather than as a
    division. Creating, there is nothing to keep and the API divides by the category's rule,
    or by the rule the middle option names. Editing, the shares the expense already has are
    kept unless somebody actually moved off By hand, which the edit dialog sends as an ask to
    divide it again; silence on an edit has meant "keep them" since a bulk pass that read it
    the other way re-divided 733 expenses.
*@

<MudStack Spacing="2">
    <MudStack Row="true" AlignItems="AlignItems.Center" Justify="Justify.SpaceBetween" Wrap="Wrap.Wrap">
        <MudText Typo="Typo.subtitle2" Class="font-weight-bold">Split</MudText>

        @* The app's own segmented control, not MudBlazor's toggle group: the theme
           switcher established this shape and a second, differently-drawn two-option
           selector in the same product reads as a component from somewhere else. The
           filled thumb says which side is chosen, so no tick is needed beside it. *@
        <div class="gs-seg gs-seg-labels"
             role="radiogroup"
             aria-label="How to divide it"
             style="--gs-seg-n: @Options; --gs-seg-i: @Position">
            <span class="gs-seg-thumb" aria-hidden="true"></span>

            <button type="button"
                    class="gs-seg-btn @(Chosen is Mode.Automatic ? "active" : "")"
                    role="radio"
                    aria-checked="@(Chosen is Mode.Automatic ? "true" : "false")"
                    disabled="@(!Members.Any())"
                    @onclick="@(() => OnModeChanged(Mode.Automatic))">
                Automatically
            </button>

            @* Always drawn, and disabled until the group's rules are in hand -- which is a
               round trip, and one the dialog makes while this is already on screen. Drawn
               only once they arrived, the control went from two options to three as they
               landed and resized itself under the pointer: the thumb is positioned from
               --gs-seg-n, so an option appearing moves every option. A disabled button that
               becomes enabled moves nothing. *@
            <button type="button"
                    class="gs-seg-btn @(Chosen is Mode.AllForOne ? "active" : "")"
                    role="radio"
                    aria-checked="@(Chosen is Mode.AllForOne ? "true" : "false")"
                    disabled="@(!Members.Any() || MemberRules.Count == 0)"
                    @onclick="@(() => OnModeChanged(Mode.AllForOne))">
                All for one
            </button>

            @* Only where an expense can have a bill at all, which is a fact the dialog knows
               before this renders and never changes while it is open -- a create dialog has
               nowhere to put a receipt, so offering the option there would be a control that
               is permanently off. Drawn from the start on an edit, disabled until the bill
               read lands, for the reason given above. *@
            @if (ShowByBill)
            {
                <button type="button"
                        class="gs-seg-btn @(Chosen is Mode.ByBill ? "active" : "")"
                        role="radio"
                        aria-checked="@(Chosen is Mode.ByBill ? "true" : "false")"
                        disabled="@(!Members.Any() || Bill is null || BillRuleId is null)"
                        @onclick="@(() => OnModeChanged(Mode.ByBill))">
                    By its bill
                </button>
            }

            <button type="button"
                    class="gs-seg-btn @(Chosen is Mode.ByHand ? "active" : "")"
                    role="radio"
                    aria-checked="@(Chosen is Mode.ByHand ? "true" : "false")"
                    disabled="@(!Members.Any())"
                    @onclick="@(() => OnModeChanged(Mode.ByHand))">
                By hand
            </button>
        </div>
    </MudStack>

    @if (!Members.Any())
    {
        <MudAlert Severity="Severity.Info" Dense="true">
            Pick a group first, and its members can be given a share.
        </MudAlert>
    }
    else if (Chosen is Mode.AllForOne)
    {
        @* One control, and then the same read-only list every automatic division shows:
           what this chooses is a rule, so the API divides by it and answers with the
           figures, exactly as it does for the category's own rule. *@
        <MudSelect T="Guid?"
                   Label="Who owes it?"
                   Value="@AllFor"
                   ValueChanged="@OnAllForChanged"
                   Variant="Variant.Outlined"
                   Margin="Margin.Dense">
            @foreach (var rule in MemberRules)
            {
                <MudSelectItem T="Guid?" Value="@rule.Id" @key="rule.Id">
                    @NameOf(rule)
                </MudSelectItem>
            }
        </MudSelect>

        @Divided
    }
    else if (Chosen is Mode.ByBill)
    {
        @* The lines, and then the same read-only list every automatic division shows: this
           names a rule like the two above it, and the API divides by it and answers with the
           figures. Nothing here is applied separately -- see BillDivision. *@
        @if (Bill is { } bill)
        {
            <BillDivision Receipt="bill"
                          TransactionId="TransactionId"
                          GroupId="GroupId"
                          Amount="Amount"
                          ReceiptChanged="OnBillChanged" />
        }

        @Divided
    }
    else if (Chosen is Mode.ByHand)
    {
        @* One field per person, and nothing repeating them below: what is typed here is
           what will be stored, so there is nothing for a second list to add. *@
        <MudStack Spacing="1">
            @foreach (var member in Members)
            {
                <MudNumericField T="decimal"
                                 @key="member.Id"
                                 Value="@ShareOf(member.Id)"
                                 ValueChanged="@(share => SetShare(member.Id, share))"
                                 Label="@member.Label()"
                                 Variant="Variant.Outlined"
                                 Margin="Margin.Dense"
                                 Adornment="Adornment.Start"
                                 AdornmentIcon="@Icons.Material.Filled.AttachMoney"
                                 HideSpinButtons="true"
                                 Immediate="true"
                                 Format="N2"/>
            }
        </MudStack>

        <MudAlert Severity="@(Assigned ? Severity.Success : Severity.Warning)" Dense="true">
            @if (Assigned)
            {
                <text>Every cent of @Amount.ToMoney() is assigned.</text>
            }
            else if (Remaining > 0)
            {
                <text>@Remaining.ToMoney() still to assign.</text>
            }
            else
            {
                <text>@((-Remaining).ToMoney()) more than the expense comes to.</text>
            }
        </MudAlert>

        @* Only once the column adds up. Until then the line above is saying the same
           thing more usefully, and two warnings about one problem read as two problems --
           but a set that sums and is still refused (naming somebody who is not in the
           group, say) would otherwise be silent until the Save button. *@
        @if (Assigned && PreviewError is { Length: > 0 } refused)
        {
            <MudAlert Severity="Severity.Warning" Dense="true">@refused</MudAlert>
        }
    }
    else
    {
        @Divided
    }
</MudStack>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// The division as the API worked it out, which is what both automatic answers
    /// show: the category's rule, and the rule the expense names for itself. One
    /// fragment rather than two copies, because the only difference between those two
    /// is which rule the dialog asked about.
    /// </summary>
    private RenderFragment Divided => @<text>
        @* Worked out by the API, which is the same code the save runs -- so this is what
           will be stored and not a second copy of the arithmetic that has to agree with
           it. Said out loud, because "as the category says" is exactly the case where
           nobody can tell what it says without being told. *@
        <MudText Typo="Typo.caption">@Explanation</MudText>

        @if (Superseded is { } changed)
        {
            @* What it does, and what the two options here do about it. The remedy this
               used to offer -- "file it under the category again" -- could not work: an
               expense keeps the version it was written under for as long as that version
               belongs to the rule its category names, so re-picking the same category
               reaches the same version it already has. *@
            <MudAlert Severity="Severity.Info" Dense="true">
                The rule has changed since this was recorded. It stays divided the way it
                was on @changed.ToLocalTime().ToString("d MMM yyyy"), and
                <strong>Automatically</strong> divides it again by that same version rather
                than by the rule as it stands now &mdash; nobody correcting an expense is
                re-billed under a division agreed afterwards. To set the shares yourself,
                choose <strong>By hand</strong>.
            </MudAlert>
        }

        @if (Awaiting is { Length: > 0 })
        {
            <MudText Typo="Typo.caption">@Awaiting</MudText>
        }
        else if (PreviewError is { Length: > 0 })
        {
            <MudAlert Severity="Severity.Warning" Dense="true">@PreviewError</MudAlert>
        }
        else if (Preview is null)
        {
            <MudText Typo="Typo.caption">Working it out…</MudText>
        }
        else
        {
            <div class="gs-list">
                @foreach (var share in Preview.Splits)
                {
                    <div class="gs-row" @key="share.UserId" style="cursor: default; padding: 8px 4px">
                        <Avatar Name="@share.UserName" Size="sm"/>
                        <div style="min-width: 0">
                            <div class="title">
                                <span>@share.UserName</span>
                                @if (share.IsPendingInvitee)
                                {
                                    <span class="gs-tag">invited</span>
                                }
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        @* What it was, where an edit has moved it. The consequence of
                           changing an amount belongs beside the new figure and before the
                           save, not in the ledger afterwards. Only where it actually moved:
                           a struck-through copy of an unchanged number is noise.

                           Both figures in one cell. The row is a three-column grid and this
                           put four children in it, so on every row an edit touched the new
                           amount -- the one the whole panel is about -- wrapped to a line of
                           its own under the name, while the old one kept the column. *@
                        <span class="gs-change">
                            @if (Was(share.UserId) is { } was && was != share.Amount)
                            {
                                <span class="gs-was gs-num">@was.ToMoney()</span>
                            }

                            <Money Value="share.Amount"/>
                        </span>
                    </div>
                }
            </div>
        }
    </text>;

    /// <summary>
    /// Everyone who may be given a share: the group's members, and the people it has
    /// invited and is waiting on -- who are marked as such on their field, since a share
    /// against somebody who has not joined is an ordinary share and not an ordinary member.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public IReadOnlyList<UserInfo> Members { get; set; } = [];

    /// <summary>What the shares have to add up to.</summary>
    [Parameter] public decimal Amount { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Who paid. Used only to decide who carries the odd cent in the starting point below,
    /// which is the same person the API's own division would give it to.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public Guid? PayerId { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The rules this group holds one of per member -- all of it is for them -- which are
    /// what the middle option picks between. Empty turns the option off.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Rules and not people, because what the expense ends up carrying is a rule's id: the
    /// division is the API's to work out, and an expense that named a person instead would
    /// be the app deciding what "all for Ana" divides into.
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter] public IReadOnlyList<SplitRuleResponse> MemberRules { get; set; } = [];

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether "By its bill" is one of the options at all.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// A property of the dialog and not of the expense: a create dialog has nowhere to put a
    /// receipt, so the option would be permanently off there. Settled before the first render
    /// and constant afterwards, which is what keeps the control from resizing under the
    /// pointer -- whether there is <em>actually</em> a bill is <see cref="Bill"/>, and that
    /// only enables or disables the button.
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter] public bool ShowByBill { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The expense's itemised bill, when it has one.</summary>
    [Parameter] public ReceiptResponse? Bill { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The group's "divide it by the bill" rule, which is what that option names.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// One, because a group is given exactly one and cannot write a second: the rule holds no
    /// settings for a second to differ by. That is what lets this be an id rather than a set
    /// to search -- groups used to hold several, and reading the first meant an expense on
    /// another of them matched nothing and lit "All for one" over an empty picker.
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter] public Guid? BillRuleId { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The expense the bill belongs to, so a line's rule can be written to it.</summary>
    [Parameter] public Guid TransactionId { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The group whose rules the bill's lines divide by.</summary>
    [Parameter] public Guid? GroupId { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Raised when a line of the bill was pointed at a different rule.</summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<ReceiptResponse> BillChanged { get; set; }

    private Task OnBillChanged(ReceiptResponse bill) => BillChanged.InvokeAsync(bill);

    /// <summary>
    /// The rule the expense names for itself, or null to divide the way its category does.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public Guid? SplitRuleId { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public EventCallback<Guid?> SplitRuleIdChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The stated division, or null to leave it to the dialog. Null is not "no shares"; it
    /// is "don't state any", which is a different instruction and the ordinary one.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Non-null is also what puts the control on "By hand", so an edit dialog that opens on
    /// the expense's own shares opens by handing them in here. Whether it should is the
    /// dialog's question and not this component's: the amounts do not say who chose them.
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter] public IReadOnlyList<SplitInput>? Value { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public EventCallback<IReadOnlyList<SplitInput>?> ValueChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// What to start from when the person takes the division over: the expense's current
    /// shares, on an edit. Absent on a create, where there is nothing stored yet and the
    /// even starting point below is used instead.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public IReadOnlyList<SplitInput>? Stored { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// What each person holds today, so the preview can show what an edit moves.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The same list as <see cref="Stored"/> in practice, and a separate parameter because
    /// the two are asked for different reasons: that one is where a hand-split starts from,
    /// this one is what the new figures are being compared against. A create passes neither
    /// -- there is nothing an expense that does not exist yet used to be.
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter] public IReadOnlyList<SplitInput>? Before { get; set; }

    /// <summary>What one person's share was before this edit, when it is known.</summary>
    private decimal? Was(Guid userId) =>
        Before?.FirstOrDefault(split => split.UserId == userId)?.Amount;

    /// <summary>
    /// What the API says this comes to, which is what the automatic division is shown as.
    /// Null while the answer is still in flight.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Passed in rather than fetched here, because the preview covers the whole expense --
    /// its amount, its payer, its group -- and this component knows about the division
    /// only. The dialog owns the request; this owns how the answer reads.
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter] public SplitPreviewResponse? Preview { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Why there is no preview, when the API refused to give one.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? PreviewError { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// What the expense is still missing before a division can be asked for at all, when
    /// it is missing something.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Distinct from <see cref="PreviewError"/>, which is a refusal of a question that was
    /// asked. A dialog opens with no amount and no name, and asking anyway returned a
    /// validation failure that reached the person as an amber warning about shares not
    /// adding up -- on a form they had not touched. Nothing is wrong yet; it is just early.
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter] public string? Awaiting { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The shares as this component currently holds them, seeded from the parameter and
    /// replaced by what the person types.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Held separately because a component may not write to its own parameter: both dialogs
    /// happen to hand the same list straight back, so it worked, but any re-render of the
    /// parent between the local write and the parent's own would have reverted whatever had
    /// just been typed -- with the figure still on screen.
    /// </remarks>
    private IReadOnlyList<SplitInput>? _shares;

    protected override void OnParametersSet() => _shares = Value;

    /// <summary>
    /// The answers to "how is this divided", in the order the control draws them.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The order here is the order on screen. The thumb is placed from the position within
    /// <see cref="Drawn"/> rather than from the enum's own number, so an option that is not
    /// drawn does not leave a gap where it would have been.
    /// </remarks>
    private enum Mode
    {
        Automatic,
        AllForOne,
        ByBill,
        ByHand
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Which of them is true now, read off the values rather than remembered.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Stated shares first: an expense may have been recorded under a rule and then split by
    /// hand, and the hand-typed amounts are what it holds. The API says the same thing in
    /// the other direction -- stating shares clears the rule the expense named -- so the two
    /// cannot both be true for long, and the one that is true of the figures on screen wins.
    /// <para>
    /// Then the bill, before the member rules, because both are "the expense names a rule"
    /// and only the id tells them apart.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// And the bill again, for the expense that does not name it. Nearly none of them do:
    /// the division comes from the category -- "Takeaway" points at the bill rule -- so the
    /// expense names nothing and the id test above says nothing either. Reading the id alone
    /// opened every one of those on "Automatically", which is true in the sense that the
    /// category decides, and useless: what somebody wants to see on an expense divided by
    /// its receipt is the receipt. <c>DividesItsExpense</c> is the API answering what
    /// actually divides it, whichever way it got there.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    private Mode Chosen =>
        _shares is not null ? Mode.ByHand
        : SplitRuleId is not null && SplitRuleId == BillRuleId ? Mode.ByBill
        : SplitRuleId is null && Bill is { DividesItsExpense: true } ? Mode.ByBill
        : SplitRuleId is not null ? Mode.AllForOne
        : Mode.Automatic;

    /// <summary>
    /// The options actually on screen, in order.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Whether the bill is among them is settled before the first render and does not move
    /// afterwards: what the control is made of cannot depend on a read that is still in
    /// flight, or it changes shape while somebody is looking at it. That is also why the
    /// button is drawn disabled rather than added late once the bill arrives.
    /// </remarks>
    private IReadOnlyList<Mode> Drawn => ShowByBill
        ? [Mode.Automatic, Mode.AllForOne, Mode.ByBill, Mode.ByHand]
        : [Mode.Automatic, Mode.AllForOne, Mode.ByHand];

    private int Options => Drawn.Count;

    /// <summary>Which slot the lit one is in.</summary>
    private int Position => Math.Max(0, Drawn.ToList().IndexOf(Chosen));

    private decimal Remaining => Amount - (_shares?.Sum(split => split.Amount) ?? 0m);

    private bool Assigned => Remaining == 0m;

    /// <summary>
    /// When the version that divided it is no longer what the rule says. Only ever set on
    /// an edit, and only when the rule has been changed since the expense was written.
    /// </summary>
    private DateTimeOffset? Superseded => Preview?.RuleSupersededAt;

    /// <summary>
    /// Why the numbers are what they are, in one line. The rule when there is one, and
    /// otherwise the thing an absent rule means -- which is a real answer and not a
    /// fallback, and reads better than leaving the caption out.
    /// </summary>
    private string Explanation => Preview?.RuleName is { Length: > 0 } rule
        ? $"Divided by the \"{rule}\" rule."
        : "Divided evenly between everyone in the group.";

    private decimal ShareOf(Guid userId) =>
        _shares?.FirstOrDefault(split => split.UserId == userId)?.Amount ?? 0m;

    /// <summary>
    /// Moves to one of the three, sending only what actually changed.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Both answers can move at once -- going from a hand-split to a named rule clears the
    /// shares and sets an id -- and each of them costs the dialog a round trip, so a pick
    /// that left one of them where it was must not announce it anyway.
    /// </remarks>
    private async Task OnModeChanged(Mode mode)
    {
        if (mode == Chosen)
            return;

        var shares = mode is Mode.ByHand ? StartingPoint() : null;

        var rule = mode switch
        {
            Mode.AllForOne => Suggested(),
            // One rule, not a suggestion: the group holds exactly one of these.
            Mode.ByBill => BillRuleId,
            _ => null
        };

        _shares = shares;

        if (rule != SplitRuleId)
            await SplitRuleIdChanged.InvokeAsync(rule);

        await ValueChanged.InvokeAsync(shares);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Which member's rule the middle option opens on: whoever paid, when they have one.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// A person has to be chosen for the option to mean anything, and opening on nobody
    /// would show a preview of the category's division under a control claiming otherwise.
    /// The payer is the one answer that is always right about itself -- "this one was for
    /// me" is the commonest thing it says -- and it is one click from any of the others.
    /// </remarks>
    private Guid? Suggested() =>
        MemberRules.FirstOrDefault(rule => rule.AllForUserId == PayerId)?.Id
        ?? MemberRules.FirstOrDefault()?.Id;

    /// <summary>The rule picked in the middle option, as the select reads it.</summary>
    private Guid? AllFor =>
        MemberRules.Any(rule => rule.Id == SplitRuleId) ? SplitRuleId : null;

    private Task OnAllForChanged(Guid? ruleId) => SplitRuleIdChanged.InvokeAsync(ruleId);

    /// <summary>
    /// What to call one of those rules: the member it is for, and not the name the rule was
    /// provisioned under.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The name is written once, when somebody joins, out of whatever profile they had
    /// then -- so a member who filled theirs in afterwards is still "All for ana@..." in the
    /// listing. The member is on the row as an id, and the people are already here.
    /// </remarks>
    private string NameOf(SplitRuleResponse rule) =>
        Members.FirstOrDefault(member => member.Id == rule.AllForUserId)?.Label() ?? rule.Name;

    private async Task SetShare(Guid userId, decimal share)
    {
        var shares = Members
            .Select(member => new SplitInput
            {
                UserId = member.Id,
                Amount = member.Id == userId ? share : ShareOf(member.Id)
            })
            .ToList();

        _shares = shares;
        await ValueChanged.InvokeAsync(_shares);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// What the fields hold the moment the person takes the division over.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The expense's own shares when it has some, which is the useful case on an edit:
    /// they came from the API, so they are already right, and the person is here to change
    /// one of them rather than all of them. A member who joined since is included at
    /// nothing, so they can be given a share without the list being wrong about who is in
    /// the group.
    /// <para>
    /// Otherwise an even division, truncated to the cent with the odd pennies going to
    /// whoever paid. That mirrors what the API does, but it is not relied on to: it is a
    /// starting point somebody is about to edit, and the only property it actually needs
    /// is the one the remaining line above checks -- that it adds up.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    private List<SplitInput> StartingPoint()
    {
        if (Stored is { Count: > 0 })
        {
            return Members
                .Select(member => new SplitInput
                {
                    UserId = member.Id,
                    Amount = Stored.FirstOrDefault(split => split.UserId == member.Id)?.Amount ?? 0m
                })
                .ToList();
        }

        if (Members.Count == 0)
            return [];

        var each = Math.Truncate(Amount / Members.Count * 100m) / 100m;

        // Whoever paid, when they are in the group at all; otherwise the first member, so
        // that the shares add up either way and nobody has to hunt for a stray cent.
        var carriesTheRemainder = Members.Any(member => member.Id == PayerId)
            ? PayerId
            : Members[0].Id;

        return Members
            .Select(member => new SplitInput
            {
                UserId = member.Id,
                Amount = member.Id == carriesTheRemainder
                    ? Amount - each * (Members.Count - 1)
                    : each
            })
            .ToList();
    }
}
